export default {"id":"m4l-level-12","number":12,"title":"Module 12","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"12.1","modules":[{"id":"m4l-m-12-1-1","code":"12.1","title":"12.1","blurb":"12.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-12-1-1","title":"12.1.1: Instrument Craft — Polyphonic Voice Allocation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 12.1.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 12 / Section 12.1","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 11.4.1 - Audio Craft — Nonlinear Saturation and Scopes.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Polyphony and poly~ Architecture</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Voice Allocation, Stealing Policies, and Panic Handlers</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Polyphonic Instrument Optimization</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Construct multi-voice polyphonic synthesizer architectures using the <code>[poly~]</code> container object.","Design independent voice subpatchers incorporating <code>[in~]</code>, <code>[out~]</code>, <code>[in]</code>, <code>[out]</code>, and <code>[thispoly~]</code>.","Implement dynamic voice allocation and configure voice-stealing policies (<code>@steal 1</code>) to eliminate dropped notes during dense chord playing.","Establish dynamic DSP voice suppression using <code>[thispoly~]</code> to disable computation on silent voices.","Implement a robust MIDI Panic / All-Notes-Off reset circuit to instantly silence hung voices."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 The poly~ Engine Architecture","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"In Max for Live, polyphonic synthesis is achieved by encapsulating a single-voice DSP subpatcher inside <code>[poly~ &lt;subpatch_name&gt; &lt;voice_count&gt;]</code>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            POLY~ ENGINE ARCHITECTURE                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ notein ] (MIDI Input from Live Track)                                      |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ midiformat / midiparse / target 0 ]                                        |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | poly~ synth_voice 8 @steal 1 @args ---                                 |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  |  +----------------+  +----------------+  ...  +----------------+        |  |\n|  |  | Voice 1 (Active|  | Voice 2 (Active|       | Voice 8 (Muted |        |  |\n|  |  |  Note C3 / DSP)|  |  Note E3 / DSP)|       |  Silent / 0%CPU|        |  |\n|  |  +--------+-------+  +--------+-------+       +--------+-------+        |  |\n|  |           |                   |                        |                |  |\n|  |           +-------------------+------------------------+                |  |\n|  |                               | (Internal Voice Summing Bus)            |  |\n|  +-------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+  |\n|                                  |                                            |\n|                                  v (Summed Stereo Audio Out)                  |\n|                             [ plugout~ ]                                      |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Anatomy of a poly~ Voice Subpatcher","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A voice subpatcher requires specialized I/O and lifecycle objects:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            POLY~ VOICE OBJECT TAXONOMY                            |\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Object            | Operational Role                                              |\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `[in 1]`, `[in 2]`| Message inlets for pitch, velocity, and parameter data.       |\n| `[in~ 1]`         | Audio-rate signal inlets (for audio modulation/sidechains).   |\n| `[out~ 1]`        | Audio-rate signal outlet (automatically summed at poly~ core).|\n| `[thispoly~]`     | Manages voice allocation state:                               |\n|                   | - Ingests mute state: `mute 1` (Disables DSP), `mute 0` (On). |\n|                   | - Ingests busy state: `busy 1` (Allocated), `busy 0` (Free).  |\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Inside Voice Subpatcher (synth_voice.maxpat):\n       [in 1] (MIDI Note Tuple: Pitch, Velocity)\n          |\n          v\n       [mtof] ---&gt; [saw~] ---&gt; [svf~] ---&gt; [*~ VCA] ---&gt; [out~ 1]\n                                              ^\n       [adsr~ 10 100 0.7 200] ================+\n          |\n          +---- (Audio Envelope Output)\n          |\n          v\n     [thispoly~]  &lt;-- Automatically sets Busy/Mute based on ADSR envelope level!</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Voice Stealing and MIDI Panic Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"4.1 Voice Stealing Policy (@steal 1)","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When an 8-voice synth plays a 9th note:"},{"type":"list","items":["Without <code>@steal 1</code>: The 9th note is ignored and dropped completely.","With <code>@steal 1</code>: <code>poly~</code> finds the oldest sounding voice (or the voice closest to zero amplitude in its release phase), abruptly fades it out, and re-allocates it to the incoming 9th note seamlessly."],"ordered":false},{"type":"heading","text":"4.2 Panic / All-Notes-Off Handler","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"If external MIDI controllers disconnect or clip loops truncate abruptly, voices can remain stuck on."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Solution:</strong> A Panic trigger that broadcasts <code>target 0, mute 1 0, stop, clear</code> to all <code>poly~</code> instances simultaneously."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       [ Panic Button / CC 123 (All Notes Off) ]\n                         |\n                         v\n       +------------------------------------+\n       |   target 0, stop, mute 1 0, midievent 0 123 0   |\n       +-----------------+------------------+\n                         |\n                         v\n       +------------------------------------+\n       |    poly~ synth_voice 8 @steal 1    |  &lt;-- Immediately silences all voices\n       +------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct an 8-voice polyphonic synthesizer using <code>poly~</code>, with dynamic DSP voice suppression, voice stealing, and a master panic reset."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI track, insert a <strong>Max Instrument</strong>, and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Build the Voice Subpatcher:","Create a new subpatcher or save an external abstraction named <code>poly_voice.maxpat</code>.","Inside <code>poly_voice</code>:","Ingest pitch and velocity from <code>[in 1]</code>.","Route pitch → <code>[mtof]</code> → <code>[saw~]</code>.","Route velocity through <code>[&gt; 0]</code> gate into <code>[adsr~ 10 200 0.6 300]</code>.","Multiply <code>[saw~]</code> by <code>[adsr~]</code> using <code>[*~]</code>.","Connect <code>[adsr~]</code> envelope output into the leftmost inlet of <code>[thispoly~]</code> (automatically handles DSP muting and busy state).","Route VCA audio into <code>[out~ 1]</code>.","Main Patcher Assembly:","On the root canvas, instantiate <code>[poly~ poly_voice 8 @steal 1]</code>.","Ingest MIDI from Live using <code>[notein]</code> → <code>[pack 0 0]</code>.","Route note tuples into <code>[midiformat]</code> → inlet 1 of <code>[poly~ poly_voice 8]</code>.","Connect outlet 1 of <code>[poly~]</code> through a master gain stage <code>[*~ 0.3]</code> → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Panic Circuit:","Create a <code>[live.button]</code> named <code>Panic</code>.","Connect to Message Box: <code>[target 0, stop, mute 1 0(</code> → inlet 1 of <code>[poly~]</code>.","Add <code>Panic</code> and master volume to Presentation Mode and save as <code>Polyphonic_Synth_Lab.amxd</code>.","Return to Live: Play complex 8-note chords and dense arpeggios. Confirm stable polyphonic voice allocation, voice stealing without audio drops, and instant silence on Panic."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Missing <code>thispoly~</code> Muting:</strong> Forgetting to connect <code>adsr~</code> to <code>thispoly~</code>. All 8 voices will compute oscillators and filters continuously, multiplying CPU consumption by 8 even when zero notes are playing.","<strong>Missing <code>@steal 1</code> Attribute:</strong> Omitting <code>@steal 1</code> on <code>poly~</code>. When playing fast piano or pad passages with a sustain pedal, notes beyond the voice limit will drop silently.","<strong>Hardcoded Monophonic Variable Collisions:</strong> Using global <code>[send]</code> or <code>[value]</code> objects inside a <code>poly~</code> voice subpatch. Setting a variable in Voice 1 will overwrite Voice 2. Inside <code>poly~</code>, always use <code>inlet</code>/<code>outlet</code> or <code>#0</code> / <code>---</code> local prefixes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the operational mechanism by which <code>[thispoly~]</code> suppresses DSP calculation on silent voices.","[ ] Explain the voice-stealing behavior of <code>[poly~]</code> when <code>@steal 1</code> is engaged versus when it is disabled.","[ ] Construct a MIDI Panic reset message string and explain its effect on active polyphonic voices."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Polyphony, Voice Allocation, and thispoly~</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Polyphony &amp; Voice Architecture</em>, pp. 74–78. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Instrument Optimization</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-12-2-2","code":"12.2","title":"12.2","blurb":"12.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-12-2-1","title":"12.2.1: Instrument Craft — Internal Modulation Systems","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 12.2.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 12 / Section 12.2","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 12.1.1 - Instrument Craft — Polyphonic Voice Allocation.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Internal Modulation and Undo History</em>.","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Modulation Matrices and Signal Crossbars</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Macro Mapping and Update Throttling</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Construct multi-destination internal modulation matrices mapping LFOs and envelopes to pitch, filter cutoff, wavetable position, and amplifier stages.","Route modulation signals without polluting Ableton Live's host undo history by enforcing <strong>Hidden Parameter Visibility</strong>.","Implement Macro-style grouped controls that scale and offset multiple internal parameters simultaneously.","Throttle high-frequency automation output using <strong>Update Limit</strong> and <code>[speedlim]</code> to prevent host UI stalls.","Build tempo-synced multi-waveform LFO modulators with phase-reset capabilities."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Modulation Matrix Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A modulation matrix routes multiple modulation sources (LFOs, Envelopes, Velocity, Key Tracking) to multiple synthesis destinations (Pitch, Cutoff, Resonance, Pan, VCA):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       INTERNAL MODULATION MATRIX TOPOLOGY                     |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ Sources ]                         [ Depth Matrix ]        [ Destinations ] |\n|                                                                               |\n|  LFO 1 (Synced Phasor) -----\\         [ *~ Depth 1 ] ------&gt; Pitch Mod In     |\n|                              \\       /                                        |\n|  LFO 2 (Free Sine) -----------\\-----/-- [ *~ Depth 2 ] ----&gt; Filter Cutoff In |\n|                                \\   /                                          |\n|  Env 2 (Filter ADSR) -----------\\-/---- [ *~ Depth 3 ] ----&gt; Wavetable Scan   |\n|                                  X                                            |\n|  Velocity (0 - 127) ------------/-\\---- [ *~ Depth 4 ] ----&gt; VCA Volume       |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.3 Protecting Host Undo History (The Modulation Needle Rule)","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When creating moving visual indicators (e.g., visual needles or animated bars showing modulated cutoff frequency):"},{"type":"list","items":["Never route the output of an LFO or envelope directly into a standard <code>live.dial</code> set to <em>Automated and Stored</em>.","Doing so generates an undo step on every clock tick (50–1000 times/sec), obliterating the user's undo queue.","<strong>The Solution:</strong> Set the parameter's visibility to <strong><code>Hidden</code></strong> in the Inspector. The dial moves visually to reflect modulation without generating undo events."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Automation Throttling and Update Limits","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When an internal algorithm (such as a random generative sequencer or envelope tracker) sends control messages out to Live parameters:"},{"type":"list","items":["In the Inspector for the parameter, configure <strong>Defer Automation Output</strong> to <code>Enabled</code>.","Set <strong>Update Limit</strong> to <code>10–20 ms</code>.","This caps outgoing message transmission to a maximum of 50–100 Hz, preserving host UI responsiveness during high-speed modulation."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a flexible internal modulation matrix inside a synthesizer instrument, routing a tempo-synced LFO and an ADSR envelope to Filter Cutoff and Oscillator Pitch with zero undo history pollution."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, open the <code>Polyphonic_Synth_Lab.amxd</code> in the Max editor.","Create Modulation Source 1 (Tempo-Synced LFO):","<code>[phasor~ 4n @lock 1]</code> → <code>[cos~]</code> (Produces a clean -1.0 to +1.0 sine wave).","Create Modulation Source 2 (Filter Envelope):","Instantiate <code>[adsr~ 20 300 0.2 400]</code>.","Create Depth Controls:","<code>[live.dial LFO_to_Cutoff]</code> (Float, <code>-1.0 to +1.0</code>, Bipolar, Initial: <code>0.0</code>).","<code>[live.dial Env_to_Cutoff]</code> (Float, <code>0.0 to 1.0</code>, Unipolar, Initial: <code>0.5</code>).","<code>[live.dial LFO_to_Pitch]</code> (Float, <code>0.0 to 12.0 Semitones</code>, Initial: <code>0.0</code>).","Matrix Summation:","Scale LFO by <code>LFO_to_Cutoff</code> using <code>[*~]</code>.","Scale Filter Envelope by <code>Env_to_Cutoff</code> using <code>[*~]</code>.","Sum both modulation signals with <code>[+~]</code>.","Apply Modulation to Filter Cutoff:","Ingest base cutoff frequency (e.g., 1000 Hz).","Multiply modulation sum by cutoff frequency range and sum with base cutoff.","Clamp final cutoff to valid range: <code>[clip~ 20.0 20000.0]</code>.","Create Modulation Display:","Instantiate <code>[live.dial Mod_Monitor]</code>.","In the Inspector: Set <strong>Parameter Visibility</strong> to <strong><code>Hidden</code></strong>.","Connect sampled cutoff modulation into <code>[live.dial Mod_Monitor]</code> for visual animation.","Add matrix controls to Presentation Mode and save as <code>Modulation_Matrix_Synth.amxd</code>.","In Live: Engage playback and verify that LFO and envelope modulate filter timbre simultaneously without generating undo clutter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unconstrained Pitch Modulation:</strong> Routing unscaled LFO signals directly into frequency inlets without converting semitones to frequency ratio multipliers. An unscaled +1.0 LFO added to 440 Hz only modulates pitch by 1 Hz (inaudible), whereas multiplied as an exponential semitone offset it yields musical vibrato.","<strong>Missing Clip Clamping on Summed Modulation:</strong> Summing multiple LFOs and envelopes without a <code>[clip~ 20 20000]</code> barrier. When multiple modulators peak simultaneously, cutoff can exceed Nyquist or drop below 0 Hz, crashing the filter.","<strong>Leaving Modulation Indicators Set to \"Automated\":</strong> Setting an animated visual knob to Automated visibility, corrupting project undo memory within seconds."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram a 2-source, 2-destination modulation matrix with independent bipolar depth controls.","[ ] Explain the mandatory configuration required for UI dials that display continuous real-time modulation.","[ ] Configure parameter Update Limits to throttle high-frequency message transmission."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Modulation Networks and Signal Crossbars</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Internal Modulation &amp; Undo Safety</em>, pp. 79–83. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Modulation Topology</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-12-3-3","code":"12.3","title":"12.3","blurb":"12.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-12-3-1","title":"12.3.1: Instrument Craft — Sample Playback and","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 12.3.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 12 / Section 12.3","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 12.2.1 - Instrument Craft — Internal Modulation Systems.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Audio Buffers and Sample Playback</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Sample Dependencies and Freezing</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Buffer Naming and File Handling</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Allocate and manage audio sample memory using <code>[buffer~]</code> with mandatory local namespace isolation (<code>---</code>).","Construct a drag-and-drop sample ingestion interface using <code>[live.drop]</code>.","Implement pitch-transposed sample playback using <code>[play~]</code>, <code>[groove~]</code>, or <code>[wave~]</code> with playback speed ratios.","Calculate pitch transposition speed ratios based on root key offsets: Speed = 2^ Pitch - Root12.","Account for sample file dependency bundling during device distribution."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Sample Playback Architecture","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Sample-based instruments ingest external audio files (WAV, AIFF, FLAC) into random-access memory (RAM) and read them back at variable sample rates:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         SAMPLE PLAYBACK SIGNAL GRAPH                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ live.drop ] (User drops sample file from Live Browser or Desktop)          |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       | (File Path String: \"C:/Samples/Kick.wav\")                             |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | [replace $1( Message ]                                                  |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       |                                       |\n|                                       v                                       |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | buffer~ ---sample_mem 10000 2 (Allocates 10 sec stereo RAM buffer)      |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       ^                                       |\n|                                       | (Memory Pointer Reference)            |\n|  [ notein Pitch ]                     |                                       |\n|       |                               |                                       |\n|       v                               |                                       |\n|  [ Speed Ratio Math ]                 |                                       |\n|  Speed = 2^((Pitch - Root)/12)        |                                       |\n|       |                               |                                       |\n|       v                               |                                       |\n|  [ sig~ Speed ]                       |                                       |\n|       |                               |                                       |\n|       v                               |                                       |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|  | groove~ ---sample_mem @loop 0 (Sample-accurate playback engine)         |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       |                                       |\n|                                       v (Stereo Sample Audio)                 |\n|                                  [ plugout~ ]                                 |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Pitch-to-Speed Ratio Mathematics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To transpose a sample across a keyboard, the playback speed of the audio pointer must scale exponentially relative to its original recorded Root Key:"},{"type":"prose","html":"Speed Ratio = 2^ P - R12"},{"type":"prose","html":"Where:"},{"type":"list","items":["P is the incoming MIDI note number (e.g., 60 = C3).","R is the sample Root Key parameter (e.g., 60).","If P = R → Speed = 2^0 = 1.0 (Original Pitch).","If P = R + 12 → Speed = 2^1 = 2.0 (One Octave Up / Double Speed).","If P = R - 12 → Speed = 2^-1 = 0.5 (One Octave Down / Half Speed)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. The [live.drop] Drag-and-Drop Interface","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <code>[live.drop]</code> object provides a native drag-and-drop drop zone on the device front panel:"},{"type":"list","items":["Accepts files dragged directly from Live's browser, the macOS Finder, or Windows Explorer.","Supported file types: WAV, AIFF, FLAC, OGG, MP3.","Emits the absolute path of the dropped file.","Formats path into a <code>[replace $1(</code> message to load the file into <code>[buffer~ ---sample_mem]</code>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a chromatic one-shot sample playback instrument with drag-and-drop sample loading, automatable root key tuning, start-point offset, and ADSR amplitude enveloping."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI Track, insert a <strong>Max Instrument</strong>, and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Allocate Sample Memory:","Create <code>[buffer~ ---sample_mem 10000 2]</code> (10-second stereo buffer with <code>---</code> local naming).","Implement Drag-and-Drop Loading:","Create <code>[live.drop @types WAVE AIFF]</code>.","Connect outlet of <code>[live.drop]</code> → <code>[replace 1(</code>\\rightarrow$ inlet of <code>[buffer~ ---sample_mem]</code>.","Pitch Transposition &amp; Playback Engine:","Ingest MIDI from <code>[notein]</code>.","Create <code>[live.dial Root_Key]</code> (Int, <code>0 - 127</code>, default: <code>60</code>).","Subtract Root Key from incoming pitch: <code>[- 60]</code>.","Calculate speed ratio: Divide by <code>12.0</code> → <code>[expr pow(2.0\\, f1)]</code>\\rightarrow$ <code>[sig~ 1.0]</code>.","Instantiate playback engine: <code>[groove~ ---sample_mem @loop 0]</code>.","Connect speed signal to inlet 1 of <code>[groove~]</code>.","Retrigger on Note-On:","Route velocity through <code>[&gt; 0]</code> gate → message <code>[0(</code> → inlet 1 of <code>[groove~]</code> (resets sample playhead to start).","Amplitude Enveloping:","Route velocity gate to <code>[adsr~ 5 200 0.8 200]</code>.","Multiply Left and Right audio outlets of <code>[groove~]</code> by the envelope signal using <code>[*~]</code>.","Route to <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Add <code>live.drop</code>, <code>Root_Key</code>, and envelope controls to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>Sample_Player_Lab.amxd</code>.","In Live, drag an audio sample into the <code>live.drop</code> zone: Play notes on a keyboard and confirm polyphonic chromatic transposition across multiple octaves."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Global <code>buffer~</code> Collisions:</strong> Omitting the <code>---</code> prefix on <code>buffer~</code> (<code>[buffer~ sample_mem]</code>). When multiple instances of the instrument are inserted on different tracks, loading a sample in Instance 2 overwrites Instance 1's audio buffer immediately.","<strong>Missing Sample Paths on Reopening:</strong> Storing sample references as hardcoded absolute system paths. If the project is opened on another machine, the device fails to find the sample. (See Lesson 16.1.1 for dependency bundling).","<strong>Triggering <code>groove~</code> with Inverted Speed:</strong> Sending negative speed values without intending reverse playback."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Calculate the playback speed ratio required to pitch a sample down by 7 semitones.","[ ] Configure <code>[live.drop]</code> to accept WAV and AIFF files and route them into a local <code>[buffer~]</code>.","[ ] Explain why the <code>---</code> prefix is critical on memory buffer objects."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: buffer~ and groove~ Playback Architecture</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Sample Management &amp; Freezing</em>, pp. 84–88. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Memory Buffers and Local Naming</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-12-4-4","code":"12.4","title":"12.4","blurb":"12.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-12-4-1","title":"12.4.1: Instrument Craft — MPE Expressive Instruments","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 12.4.1 (Includes Gate I Assessment)","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 12 / Section 12.4","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 12.3.1 - Instrument Craft — Sample Playback and <code>buffer~</code>.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual: <em>MPE and Expressive Sound Design</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>MPE Instrument Implementation and Checklist</em>.","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 Guide: Polyphonic Expression in poly~</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Build a multi-voice <strong>MPE Polyphonic Synthesizer</strong> accepting independent per-note Glide (Pitch Bend), Press (Pressure), and Slide (CC 74) modulation streams.","Route multi-channel MPE streams into <code>[poly~]</code> voice instances using channel-indexed voice routing (<code>midievent</code> / target messages).","Map per-note pressure to individual voice VCA dynamics and per-note slide (CC 74) to individual voice filter cutoffs.","Implement automatic fallback logic for standard non-MPE MIDI controllers.","Complete <strong>Gate I (Instrument Craft Mastery)</strong> by verifying complete polyphonic stability, undo safety, and MPE articulation across a multi-voice instrument."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 MPE Instrument Architecture","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"In an MPE instrument, each active voice subpatcher computes its own independent pitch bend, pressure curve, and filter cutoff modulation based on its assigned member channel (2–16):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         MPE INSTRUMENT SIGNAL GRAPH                           |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ midiin ] (Raw Multichannel MPE Stream: Channels 2 - 16)                    |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | poly~ mpe_voice 15 @steal 1 @args ---                                   |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  |  +-------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |  | Voice Instance (Channel Index: 2)                                 |  |  |\n|  |  | - Ingests: Note C3 + Pitch Bend (+2.4 st) + Pressure + CC 74     |  |  |\n|  |  | - Audio DSP: [saw~] -&gt; [svf~ (Cutoff mod by CC74)] -&gt; [*~ (Press)]|  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------------------+---------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |                                    |                                    |  |\n|  |  +---------------------------------+---------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |  | Voice Instance (Channel Index: 3)                                 |  |  |\n|  |  | - Ingests: Note E3 + Pitch Bend (-0.5 st) + Pressure + CC 74     |  |  |\n|  |  | - Audio DSP: [saw~] -&gt; [svf~ (Cutoff mod by CC74)] -&gt; [*~ (Press)]|  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------------------+---------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |                                    |                                    |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       |                                       |\n|                                       v (Summed Stereo Audio Out)             |\n|                                  [ plugout~ ]                                 |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Per-Note Expression Routing Inside the Voice Subpatcher","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Inside <code>mpe_voice.maxpat</code>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Glide (Pitch Bend):</strong> Ingests 14-bit pitch bend (± 8192), scales to semitones (e.g. ± 48 semitones), converts to frequency multiplier, and multiplies base oscillator frequency.","<strong>Press (Channel Pressure):</strong> Ingests 0–127 pressure, scales to 0.0–1.0, and modulates amplifier envelope gain or saturation drive.","<strong>Slide (CC 74):</strong> Ingests 0–127 timbre controller, scales to 0.0–1.0, and modulates the individual voice filter cutoff."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Inside [mpe_voice]:\n  [Pitch Base] ----&gt; [ mtof ] ---&gt; [*~ Glide Ratio] ---&gt; [saw~]\n                                                           |\n  [CC 74 Slide] ---&gt; [ Scale 0-1 ] --------------------&gt; [svf~ Cutoff]\n                                                           |\n  [Pressure] ------&gt; [ Scale 0-1 ] --------------------&gt; [*~ VCA Gain]\n                                                           |\n                                                           v\n                                                        [out~ 1]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct an 8-voice MPE Polyphonic Synthesizer with per-note pitch bend glides, pressure-sensitive VCA dynamics, and per-note CC 74 filter brightness sweeps."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI track, insert a <strong>Max Instrument</strong>, and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Open Patcher Inspector → Enable <strong>MPE Supported</strong> (<code>is_mpe = 1</code>) and <strong>Open in Presentation</strong>.","Create the MPE Voice Subpatcher (<code>mpe_voice.maxpat</code>):","Ingest MIDI messages using <code>[in 1]</code>.","Parse pitch, velocity, pressure, pitch bend, and CC 74 using <code>[midiparse]</code>.","Route pitch → <code>[mtof]</code> → multiply by pitch bend ratio → <code>[saw~]</code>.","Route CC 74 (0–127) to modulate <code>[svf~]</code> cutoff frequency (100 to 12000 Hz).","Route velocity and pressure to shape <code>[adsr~]</code> and VCA multiplier <code>[*~]</code>.","Connect <code>[adsr~]</code> to <code>[thispoly~]</code> for automatic DSP suppression.","Route audio to <code>[out~ 1]</code> and <code>[out~ 2]</code>.","Main Patcher Assembly:","Instantiate <code>[poly~ mpe_voice 8 @steal 1]</code>.","Ingest raw MIDI using <code>[midiin]</code>.","Route <code>[midiin]</code> into <code>[midiselect]</code> / channel router → inlet 1 of <code>[poly~]</code>.","Connect <code>[poly~]</code> stereo outlets through master gain stage <code>[*~ 0.3]</code> → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Add master controls to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>MPE_Poly_Synthesizer.amxd</code> in <code>User Library/Presets/Instruments/Max Instrument/</code>.","Return to Live 12: In an MPE MIDI Clip, draw a chord where one note glides up by 5 semitones while another note swells in pressure and a third note opens its filter via CC 74 Slide.","Play back the clip: Confirm true per-note multidimensional expressive polyphony with zero note crosstalk."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Gate I Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!IMPORTANT] <strong>Gate I: Instrument Craft Mastery Assessment</strong> To complete Module 12 and verify advanced synthesizer instrument design competence, the student must execute and verify the following:"},{"type":"heading","text":"Practical Test Assignment:","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In a clean <code>.amxd</code> instrument:","Construct an 8-voice polyphonic synthesizer architecture utilizing <code>[poly~]</code> with <code>@steal 1</code> and <code>[thispoly~]</code> DSP muting.","Implement an internal modulation matrix where LFOs and envelopes modulate parameters without generating undo history floods (all modulation targets set to <strong>Hidden</strong> visibility).","Configure <strong>MPE Supported</strong> (<code>is_mpe = 1</code>) with per-note Glide, Press, and Slide routing.","Implement a Panic button that instantly terminates all voices.","Verification in Live:","Play a 10-finger chord on an MPE controller: Confirm zero dropped notes, flawless voice stealing, and independent per-finger pitch bending.","Trigger the Panic button: All audio must immediately cease.","Run the device for 60 seconds of dense modulation: Live's Undo history must remain 100% clean."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Global CC 74 Routing in MPE:</strong> Parsing CC 74 on Channel 1 and applying it globally across all voices. In MPE, CC 74 must be parsed per member channel (2–16) inside each individual voice instance.","<strong>Missing <code>is_mpe</code> Flag:</strong> Forgetting to enable <code>is_mpe = 1</code> in the Patcher Inspector, causing Live to strip per-note channel assignments before sending data to the device.","<strong>Unbounded Voice CPU Usage:</strong> Forgetting to connect <code>adsr~</code> to <code>thispoly~</code> inside the MPE voice, causing 15 voices to compute DSP continuously even when idle."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual: MPE and Multi-Channel Architecture</em>. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Final QA Checklist - MPE Instruments</em>, pp. 89–93. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 User Guide: MPE Synthesis and Polyphony</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
